+VLA safety spans training-time and inference-time approaches \citep{li2026vlasafety}. Inference-time methods add safety constraints during action generation: OmniGuide \citep{song2026omniguide} backpropagates SDF gradients through denoising steps, AEGIS \citep{hu2025aegis} projects actions onto control barrier function (CBF) safe sets, and SafeDiffuser \citep{xiao2025safediffuser} embeds CBF invariance into diffusion sampling. These methods handle novel obstacles without retraining but incur latency overhead and require runtime access to scene geometry. On the training side, SafeVLA \citep{zhang2025safevla} applies constrained RL fine-tuning and SCPO \citep{zhao2024scpo} enforces state-wise safety constraints; both reduce violation rates but rely on abstract cost signals rather than explicit spatial reasoning. CoFreeVLA \citep{zhai2026cofreevla} trains a binary risk estimator for dual-arm self-collision that gates unsafe actions at inference. \ours{} occupies a distinct position: SDF collision geometry is internalized into VLA weights during training, and the standard forward pass produces inherently safe actions with zero inference overhead and no runtime geometry access.

 \paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}
-RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt2} pioneered the VLA paradigm by casting actions as language tokens, and OpenVLA \citep{kim2024openvla} provided an open-source autoregressive alternative; $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} introduced continuous flow-matching action generation, which $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extended to open-world generalization with open-source weights via the openpi codebase. Recent work has explored geometric priors: GLaD \citep{guo2025glad} distills 3D geometric features into VLA hidden states for general spatial understanding, and GeoAware-VLA \citep{abouzeid2025geoaware} integrates frozen geometric vision features to improve viewpoint generalization. \ours{} differs in internalizing collision-specific SDF geometry into flow-matching weights for safety rather than general spatial robustness.
+RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt2} established the VLA paradigm by encoding actions as language tokens from a web-pretrained VLM, and OpenVLA \citep{kim2024openvla} provided an open-source autoregressive alternative. $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} replaced discrete tokenization with continuous flow-matching action generation, and $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extended this to open-world generalization with open-source weights. Recent work augments VLAs with geometric priors: GLaD \citep{guo2025glad} distills 3D features from a geometry-aware vision transformer into VLA hidden states for spatial understanding, and GeoAware-VLA \citep{abouzeid2025geoaware} integrates frozen geometric vision features for viewpoint robustness. \ours{} is complementary to these architectural advances: rather than improving general spatial reasoning, it targets collision safety by internalizing SDF geometry into flow-matching weights, and can in principle be applied to any flow-matching VLA backbone.

 \paragraph{SDF in Robotics and Guidance Distillation.}
-Signed distance representations have long been central to collision-free motion planning \citep{zucker2013chomp, schulman2014trajopt}. Neural SDF representations, pioneered by DeepSDF \citep{park2019deepsdf}, have since been adopted in manipulation planning \citep{chen2026cssdf,weng2023ngdf}, and several VLA works use a ``train-with-geometry, infer-without'' paradigm for general spatial accuracy \citep{liu2025occvla,li2025spatial,qian2025geopredict}. Separately, \citet{meng2023distillation} showed that classifier-free guidance can be distilled into diffusion model weights, and \citet{kim2026sgf} further establish that negative guidance in flow models \citep{lipman2023flow} must activate within a specific noise regime. \ours{} draws an analogy to these ideas for VLA safety: we internalize configuration-space SDF into flow-matching velocity fields through an auxiliary head discarded at inference, with a noise-aware weight $w(t)$ that instantiates the regime-specific activation principle for collision shaping.
+Signed distance fields are the standard collision representation in gradient-based motion planning \citep{zucker2013chomp, schulman2014trajopt}. DeepSDF \citep{park2019deepsdf} showed that neural networks can learn continuous SDF representations, and subsequent work applies neural SDFs to manipulation planning \citep{chen2026cssdf,weng2023ngdf}. Several VLA works adopt a ``train-with-geometry, infer-without'' paradigm: OccVLA \citep{liu2025occvla} uses occupancy prediction as an auxiliary training signal for driving, Spatial Forcing \citep{li2025spatial} aligns VLA features with 3D representations, and GeoPredict \citep{qian2025geopredict} adds 3D Gaussian supervision for manipulation precision. All three target general spatial accuracy rather than collision safety. On the generative modeling side, \citet{meng2023distillation} demonstrated that classifier-free guidance can be distilled into diffusion model weights and discarded at inference. \citet{kim2026sgf} extend this to flow-matching policies and show that guidance signals are effective only in specific noise regimes \citep{lipman2023flow}. \ours{} adapts both principles: SDF collision guidance is internalized into flow-matching weights via an auxiliary head discarded at inference, and a noise-aware weight $w(t)$ restricts collision shaping to the low-noise regime where geometric predictions are reliable.


